Neither the note nor its Source authoring pays any original case.

The supplied criterion
----------------------

Let F be a field, let V=F^n carry a right action of a group G, and let
H=<g_0,...,g_{k-1}> be a subgroup of G. Suppose A is an r-by-n matrix
and R is an n-by-r matrix, with 1<=r<n, satisfying

  AR=I_r.

For each j assume the full matrix identity

  A g_j = (A g_j R) A.                              (1)

Finally suppose that z is a known member of G and that, for one row a_i
of A, its actual image u=a_i z satisfies

  u != (uR)A.                                      (2)

Then H is a proper subgroup of G.

Proof. If cA=0, multiplying by R gives c=cAR=0. The r rows of A are
therefore independent; W=rowspan(A) has dimension r. For an arbitrary
row u, membership in W is equivalent to u=(uR)A: the displayed equality
itself puts u in W, while u=cA implies (uR)A=cARA=cA=u. In particular,
this is an exact membership test rather than a heuristic projection.

Equation (1) puts every basis-row image under g_j in W, so Wg_j is a
subspace of W. Since each g_j acts invertibly, dimensions give Wg_j=W.
Consequently W is H-invariant. If z were in H, every a_i z would belong
to W, contradicting (2). Thus z is in G\H and H!=G. No faithfulness,
semisimplicity, irreducibility, preserved bilinear form, maximal-subgroup
classification or group enumeration is needed for this implication.
The actual-member premise z in G is essential: an arbitrary matrix
outside the stabilizer of W would prove nothing about H<G.

