Untrusted acquisition
--------------------

Discovery starts at the same actual seed. It stores actual orbit rows
separately from an echelon workspace. For each stored basis row, in
queue order, it tries its four actual images and stores an image only
when elimination proves independence. The stored row is never replaced
by its normalized remainder. At most seven rows and 28 candidates are
considered. If dimension seven is reached, discovery returns
FULL_SPACE_INCONCLUSIVE and no proper-subgroup certificate.

Otherwise the queue closes at some 1<=r<=6. Its r pivot columns give a
nonsingular r-by-r minor of the actual row matrix A. Gauss-Jordan
inversion of that minor, embedded in the corresponding rows of a
7-by-r zero matrix, constructs R. The implementation checks AR=I_r in
full. It then tries only the two supplied original ambient members and
at most r actual rows for (2). If none moves the space, the outcome is
NO_SUPPLIED_AMBIENT_MOVER_INCONCLUSIVE; no ambient irreducibility or
exhaustive search is inferred. Every proposed certificate is subjected
to the same supplied-equation checker before discovery publishes it.

